From: mothershipOS
Subject: [PATCH] media: venus: key the SM8250 OPP table to the vcodec0 core clock

The SM8250 Venus OPP table lists MVS0 rates (video_cc_mvs0_clk = the source / 3: 240/338/366/444 MHz), but the
driver registers "core" (MVS0C, the source / 2) as the OPP clock. dev_pm_opp_set_rate() rounds the target on that
clock: 444 MHz became 507 MHz, which has no OPP, so every level above the lowest failed ("failed to find OPP for
freq 507000000", then "HW can't support this load") and the core stayed at 240 MHz whatever the load. Use
"vcodec0_core" as the OPP clock on SM8250, so the rate, its rounding and the required performance states match.

---
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/pm_helpers.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/pm_helpers.c
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 #include <linux/interconnect.h>
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/pm_domain.h>
 #include <linux/pm_opp.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
@@ -990,7 +991,15 @@
 	if (legacy_binding)
 		return 0;
 
-	ret = devm_pm_opp_set_clkname(dev, "core");
+	/*
+	 * SM8250: the OPP table lists the vcodec0 core clock rates (MVS0, the
+	 * source / 3: 240/338/366/444 MHz). "core" is MVS0C (the source / 2), so
+	 * every level above the lowest rounded to a rate with no OPP and the
+	 * clock could never scale up. Key the OPPs to vcodec0_core there.
+	 */
+	ret = devm_pm_opp_set_clkname(dev,
+				      of_device_is_compatible(dev->of_node, "qcom,sm8250-venus") ?
+				      "vcodec0_core" : "core");
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
